00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Mm-hmm.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:23.000
Tirzah's iPhone (2): So I don't know if I want to out of the gate say don't show it to people that are looking for all inclusive because they do have in-house coordination.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:24.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Okay.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:28.000
Tirzah's iPhone (2): So, which is one of the big priorities for people that want all-inclusive, so…

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:32.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Yeah, we could leave it neutral for now, and then if we just notice that's .

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.000
Tirzah's iPhone (2): I would be fine with that. Mm-hmm.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:41.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Yeah. Okay. And I guess we'll just keep it open if people search for competitors and happen to find our site, that's totally fine.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:46.000
Alina: Why do you have plan and plans as a negative? What am I missing there?

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:46.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Normally…

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:57.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Yeah, that's, like, kind of a generic thing, uh, just in general for different people. If they are more DIY, they likely wouldn't pay for a service in general, uh, across all industries.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:58.000
Carmen Cruickshank: But…

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:07.000
Carmen Cruickshank: if someone maybe was, like, searching for a wedding planner, and then they wanted a venue, I'm not sure they would be a lead at that moment, so I don't really want to waste ad spend.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:08.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Ah.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:11.000
Carmen Cruickshank: So yeah, that's normally why I keep it in there.

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:18.000
Tirzah's iPhone (2): You're trying to avoid the planners by using those words?

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:21.000
Carmen Cruickshank: No, I mean, that was kind of a specific example I gave.

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:22.000
Alina: I think she's trying to avoid people at that stage.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:34.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Yeah, I mean, it's not really the stage or the specific thing. That's more for, like, all industries, even if it's not wedding industry. Just people in general looking for DIY or just to plan themselves. They don't normally want to invest in a service at that time, if that makes sense.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:24.000
Alina: Is that what you're saying?

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:39.000
Alina: So it's a waste of ad spend, she's saying, at the phase that they're in.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:36.000
Whitney Hodges: Yep.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:40.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Yes.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:48.000
Whitney Hodges: Including plan or plans is more, like, indicative of somebody doing research about wedding venues in general, as opposed to…

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:41.000
Tirzah's iPhone (2): Okay.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:52.000
Whitney Hodges: actually looking for a wedding venue to get married.

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:54.000
Tirzah's iPhone (2): Yeah, I guess that makes sense. Okay.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:55.000
Tirzah's iPhone (2): Sure.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:55.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Mm-hmm.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah. Yeah.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.000
Tirzah's iPhone (2): We'll trust you on that.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Mm-hmm.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:04.000
Whitney Hodges: Any other questions about the Google Ads strategy or keywords?

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:04.000
Tirzah's iPhone (2): I don't think so.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.000
Alina: Mm-mm.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:19.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, perfect. Then I'm going to jump back into the doc and just chat in a little bit more detail about what the plan is on the meta ads side.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:09.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Perfect.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:36.000
Whitney Hodges: So, um, to kick things off, initially, with, like, a first phase, we really want to be building audiences for you on Facebook and Instagram. So, getting engagement, getting video views, and doing it really cost-effectively.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:52.000
Whitney Hodges: So, we do that through what we call client attraction campaigns. So, this is value-rich video content that is serving as an entry point for people who have never seen you on social media before. They have never visited the website before.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:18:08.000
Whitney Hodges: completely cold audiences. So, it's an introduction to your brand, an introduction to the venue. We look at it as an opportunity to really kind of put your best foot forward, and we kind of think of it through the lens of, you know, if there's one piece of content that you could put in front of an ideal client who hadn't heard of you before.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:27.000
Whitney Hodges: what would it be? So that's kind of our frame of reference as we're putting this content together. The why behind it is because we want to create more visibility, more awareness around Dogwood Reserve, and we want to do it cost-effectively. So we're doing this to grow your Facebook and Instagram retargeting.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:44.000
Whitney Hodges: audiences with the objective of engagement and video views. And what we can also do through this is really be testing what creatives and messaging are resonating with potential couples. So, to kick this off, because it is.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:19:01.000
Whitney Hodges: you know, it is a very cost-effective way to run ads. It's not necessarily lead generation yet, right? Because your lead generation campaigns will still be in the process of creating those. So we started off with just $10 a day.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:20.000
Whitney Hodges: The targeting is based on, um, interest-based audiences, right? So people who we know are engaged or are planning a wedding, um, we're gonna be targeting the locations that you serve, but we're gonna be excluding custom audiences for you, so excluding anyone who has ever seen you on Facebook or Instagram before.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:38.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, excluding anyone who has visited the website before. And in doing that, that makes sure that this particular campaign of ads, these ads, are getting brand new eyes on them every single day. Like, always new people that are being, like, pulled into.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:55.000
Whitney Hodges: the funnel. Um, we can include, you know, a link that goes to the weddings page of the website, but that's not necessarily the main objective. The objective really is to just get more visibility on the platform for you, so that we can do it in a really.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:20:02.000
Whitney Hodges: cost-effective way. Get people to actually engage with and watch these video creatives.

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:22.000
Whitney Hodges: So, as soon as, you know, we have this round of ad creatives ready for review, we'll get those sent over to you. Once you approve them, then these will be, you know, those initial campaigns that are launched on Meta. But the long-term plan, so, you know, once we have conversion ads ready for you.

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:34.000
Whitney Hodges: is to be spending, um, $1,000 a month on your meta ads. So once your conversion campaigns are ready to go, we'll reduce the campaign budget of the client attraction ads to $2 a day.

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:52.000
Whitney Hodges: And really focus the majority of the budget, $28.33 per day, on the conversion campaigns, because the goal of these is to get inquiries, to get people to, you know, fill out the form on the website to enter your funnel. So, um, this content is quite a bit more.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:21:08.000
Whitney Hodges: direct, right, in terms of, like, telling them the benefits of the venue, telling them to inquire, asking them to book their tour, uh, you know, saying, you know, click the link to learn more, and then, you know, fill out the form, and we'll set up your tour, that sort of thing, really kind of, like.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:24.000
Whitney Hodges: pushing them into that process. But along with that, you know, we want to be, you know, educating them on what the venue is all about, inviting them, like I said, to take those next steps and inquire, or to check availability, or to schedule their tour.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:41.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, oftentimes for these types of campaigns, we're incorporating, um, testimonials, real wedding footage, behind the scenes, so that's kind of future state, right, that we'll be thinking about once, um, you know, you're getting weddings, um, booked and things like that.

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:58.000
Whitney Hodges: In the meantime, what it's probably going to look like, we see collages perform really well for venues where there are lots of beautiful photos of the venue really all together. So targeting for these will be similar to the client attraction targeting.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:14.000
Whitney Hodges: It is going to be, you know, the interest-based wedding audiences and the locations that you want to target, but there won't be exclusions, right? If somebody has seen other ads of yours or engaged with you on Facebook and Instagram, we do want them to see these ads. If they've engaged with.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:27.000
Whitney Hodges: your website and visited the website, we do want to follow up and have them see these ads. So that's how it kind of becomes a mix of cold and warm audiences that are able to see these conversion campaigns.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:29.000
Alina: Okay.

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:32.000
Whitney Hodges: And then what we're doing…

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:34.000
Alina: So the warm, so the cold is for how long?

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:44.000
Whitney Hodges: So, um, you know, this would be, like, a couple of weeks, probably, where it's running at just $10 a day, and then once, you know, your conversion campaigns are approved.

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:45.000
Alina: And nothing else is running while that's running.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:46.000
Whitney Hodges: Sorry?

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:48.000
Alina: Nothing else is running while that's running.

00:22:48.000 --> 00:23:10.000
Whitney Hodges: Right, because, you know, as we're creating the ad copy and creatives, as you're approving them, we're like rolling them out campaign by campaign, right? And so, even though, you know, there wouldn't be those conversion campaigns running in the beginning, this is like laying the groundwork, laying the foundation, because it's starting to build some audiences for you on the platform and just work really well.

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:57.000
Alina: Okay.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:12.000
Whitney Hodges: cost effectively.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:13.000
Alina: Okay.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:14.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:31.000
Whitney Hodges: And then the final phase of campaigns that would be layered in would be a retargeting campaign. So, the objective of these is traffic, to get more clicks, to get more people over to the website, but these would strictly be running to.

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:55.000
Whitney Hodges: people who have engaged with you on Facebook or Instagram before, or who have visited the website before. And because in Meta's eyes, that's a very small audience, we can do this really cost-effectively at a very small daily budget, so just $3 per day. We'd be targeting all of your warm audiences, so anybody who's engaged with you on social media for the past year, anybody who's visited the website in the past.

00:23:55.000 --> 00:24:15.000
Whitney Hodges: 180 days, which I know, you know, there's not necessarily that history now, but there will be in the future. So those audiences will continue to to build up. So the types of content that we're typically utilizing in campaigns like these are just further educating, further nurturing, right? Helping to overcome objectives.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:31.000
Whitney Hodges: Giving them information to help them self-qualify, sharing more about the process of booking a wedding with you, having the wedding with you, answering frequently asked questions. So that's the goal of these. So when everything is then all running together.

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:44.000
Whitney Hodges: you have this whole funnel, this whole suite of ads, right? A variety of different creatives and copy, um, running at different stages, um, of the… the funnel. So, it's not, um, like, you'll…

00:24:44.000 --> 00:25:01.000
Whitney Hodges: if you've ever experienced this before, where it's like you just see the same one ad for a business over and over and over. And, you know, it starts to just kind of, you know, be something you scroll right past. The goal here is that, you know, brides or couples are seeing.

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:09.000
Whitney Hodges: lots of different pieces of content of you in their stories and their news feeds repeatedly, so they can continue to learn more and more.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:14.000
Alina: Cool.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:14.000
Whitney Hodges: Any questions.

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:16.000
Alina: I don't think so.

